Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Meridian Protocol
- Story & Context: Silas was the first engineer to notice the station’s needle move toward the forbidden 99.8 frequency as the clouds broke open over Black Mesa. He witnessed the mixing console fuse into a solid block of obsidian, yet the speakers began to scream a frequency that sounded like a map being torn in half. "Calculate the voltage of our departure" is the only warning he etched into the copper plating of his station before the storm swallowed the facility whole.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A glowing, abstract topographic map rendered in translucent, crackling lines of static against a pitch-black background, bioluminescent blues and violet ink bleeds.

Track 2: Vex-Code at Oakhaven Border
- Story & Context: Mara stands at the edge of Oakhaven, the rusted fence rattling in the wind as she transmits her digital confession to the cloud of electricity hovering overhead. She whispers secrets into her Shreddage Hydra, the strings sparking as she admits she was the one who jammed the transmitter during the July cyclone. "We are only coordinates in a dying broadcast" she sings, her voice heavily modulated into a glassy, robotic lament that mirrors the rain-slicked landscape.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A blurry, cinematic long-exposure of a solitary woman standing at a barb-wire fence under a dark, bruised sky, the wires shimmering with ethereal digital white noise.

Track 3: The Cartography of Charred Ground
- Story & Context: Centuries later, the ruins of the transmitter tower are treated as a relic where pilgrims leave copper trinkets to ensure the sky remembers their name. The myth says that if you stand in the center of the scorched field during a heavy pour, the earth itself begins to play the final, looped riff of the station’s last broadcast. "Did the sky ever forgive the geography" echoes through the air, vibrating the very ground beneath the feet of the listeners who have come to worship the silence after the storm.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lonely, monumental steel structure half-buried in volcanic ash and black flowers, with light pouring through the hollow architecture in beams of iridescent static.

